# APK Task Split (optional, manual only)

Task split turns an explicitly named planning target into a proposed set of small, executable APK
tasks. It prefers tracer-bullet vertical slices that each produce an independently observable
outcome over DB/API/frontend/tests fragments that cannot deliver a meaningful result alone.

It is optional and manual. It does not implement product code, does not claim or gate work, and
does not replace planning, task grill, or per-task review.

## When to use it

- Only on an explicit human request naming a target, for example: "Use apk-task-split on task
  0140", "Split this spec into APK tasks", or "Decompose the approved grill output".
- Never activate automatically. No heuristic, scanner, complexity rule, work loop, or review hook
  may invoke it, and no task may require it.
- A small task needs no split metadata, state, or gate and proceeds directly through the normal
  work, verify, review (if required), gate, and done flow.

## Inspect repository truth first

Before proposing anything, read the target and the surrounding repository state using existing
interfaces:

- `apk context <task-id> --budget <units>` and the task `## Context files` list;
- `apk task deps <task-id>` for prerequisites and dependents;
- `apk suggest-context "<description>"` for optional candidates only;
- canonical docs and existing task contracts, including obvious overlap with already-planned or
  done work.

Context output is a selection interface; read the selected files before reasoning. Required
context is never silently dropped; surface an overflow instead of hiding it. Do not dump the
whole repository or history.

## Propose a finite preview

Produce a preview of individually observable and reviewable slices. For each slice state:

- goal and observable outcome;
- narrow allowed/forbidden paths;
- bounded context files;
- acceptance criteria, verification, and required evidence;
- a reviewable boundary (what a reviewer can inspect independently);
- explicit dependency edges to other slices or existing tasks.

Then show the dependency DAG, actual parallelism, blocking edges, necessary shared
migration/schema prerequisites, and merge candidates where slicing is too fine. A technical
prerequisite is valid only when concretely necessary; do not disguise artificial horizontal
fragmentation as independent capability. Label preview edges symbolically until canonical ID
allocation returns real IDs.

## Approval before writes

Present the complete proposed structural delta, including any new task contracts, symbolic
edges, and any disposition for an unfinished parent task, with paths. Require explicit human
approval of that proposal before any write.

- No response, silence, or refusal produces no writes.
- Partial approval may be applied only when the accepted set still forms a coherent dependency
  graph; otherwise clarify the remaining choices first.
- An invocation authorizes inspection and proposals, never unapproved structural writes or
  lifecycle changes.

## Create through canonical facilities

After approval, create only the accepted contracts with existing APK facilities:

- use canonical task creation and dependency-graph validation;
- report the actual auto-assigned IDs and resolve the symbolic preview edges to them;
- respect the target's owner, state, and scope; for an unfinished parent, state its lifecycle and
  dependency disposition explicitly and honor existing-owner constraints;
- do not automatically cancel, reopen, or claim the parent, and do not silently change its
  contract;
- preserve historical done tasks and evidence; never rewrite history.

Stop before product implementation and hand the created contracts back to the normal workflow.

## Hard boundaries

- No new CLI, orchestration/runtime, task store, second state machine, or daemon.
- No automatic decomposition, backlog scanner, or recommendation engine.
- No writes before explicit approval; no lifecycle changes by implication.
- No provider/model requirement; the plain asset works with local models and compatible harnesses.
- No duplication of the task-grill or per-task review workflows.

## Relation to task grill

Task grill clarifies one task before implementation. Task split decomposes a larger target into
multiple tasks. They are separate optional tools: approved grill output is one possible input to a
split, not a prerequisite, and neither mandates the other.

## Invocation note for harnesses

This asset is plain Markdown shipped inside the installed package under
`dist/core/templates/skills/apk-task-split/SKILL.md.hbs`. A harness without native skill discovery
must read that installed asset explicitly when a human invokes task split. APK adds no installer,
model routing, provider integration, or user-global skill directory write.
